Consider a peer-to-peer (P2P) network, modeled as an overlay graph G = (V, E) of participating nodes (peers) that cooperate to provide a distributed service such as file lookup, content distribution, or routing. Typical P2P designs include: (i) unstructured overlays with flooding-based search (e.g., Gnutella-like networks), (ii) structured overlays based on distributed hash tables (e.g., Chord-, Pastry-, Kademlia-, or CAN-like networks), and (iii) hybrid or super-peer architectures.

A denial-of-service (DoS) attack against such a network is any adversarial behavior, performed by a bounded set of malicious peers or external attackers, whose goal is to degrade or prevent honest peers from receiving the intended service. The query-flood attack — in which an attacker injects an excessive number of search queries to consume bandwidth and processing — is one well-studied class of DoS attack. Other classes include, but are not limited to:
 - routing-table poisoning and Eclipse attacks (corrupting honest peers' neighbor sets);
 - Sybil attacks (an attacker controlling many pseudonymous identities);
 - index/storage poisoning attacks (injecting bogus indexes or content);
 - reflection or amplification attacks using P2P traffic against external victims;
 - churn-induced or join/leave attacks that destabilize overlay maintenance;
 - targeted attacks on specific keys, hot-spot nodes, or bootstrap services.

Problem. For each major class of DoS attack listed above (excluding query-flood attacks) and for each of the three families of P2P designs (unstructured, structured DHT-based, and hybrid/super-peer), determine:

(a) a quantitative model of the attack's effect, e.g., bounds on the fraction of honest lookups that fail, the expected latency increase, or the bandwidth overhead, as a function of the fraction f of malicious peers, network size n, and overlay parameters such as degree and routing-table size;

(b) provably effective defense mechanisms — protocols, admission-control rules, redundancy schemes, reputation/credit systems, or cryptographic constructions — together with formal guarantees on the level of service preserved under the attack model, and an analysis of their practical overhead (computation, bandwidth, and latency) for realistic deployments.

Provide either matching upper and lower bounds, or a demonstration that no defense achieving stated guarantees exists within the given attack model.

